From: turbo-boo <59400127+turbo-boo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 09:38:10 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] mmd: Apply the per-device writeback properties to zram0

Params::default() already holds one PerDeviceParams with the built-in
defaults, and load_zram_writeback_params() appended the values read from
mmd.zram.writeback.{huge_idle,idle,huge}.enabled after it.
mark_and_flush_pages() looks the entries up by device index, so zram0
always used the built-in defaults and the properties never took effect.

On the Redmi Note 10 JE (iris), whose stock 5.4 kernel only accepts
"idle" and "huge" in /sys/block/zram0/writeback, every maintenance run
therefore failed with EINVAL on "huge_idle" before writing anything back,
even with mmd.zram.writeback.huge_idle.enabled=false.

Drop the default entry before loading the per-device values.
---
  src/service.rs | 1 +
  1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/src/service.rs b/src/service.rs
index 66271ed..cc17f50 100644
--- a/src/service.rs
+++ b/src/service.rs
@@ -615,6 +615,7 @@ fn load_zram_writeback_params(num_devices: u64) -> Option<mmd::zram::writeback::
     params.min_bytes = U64Prop::ZramWritebackMinBytes.get(params.min_bytes);
     params.max_bytes = U64Prop::ZramWritebackMaxBytes.get(params.max_bytes);
     params.max_bytes_per_day = U64Prop::ZramWritebackMaxBytesPerDay.get(params.max_bytes_per_day);
+    params.per_device_params.clear();
     for idx in 0..num_devices {
         let mut pdp = mmd::zram::writeback::PerDeviceParams::default();
         pdp.huge_idle = PerDeviceBoolProp::ZramWritebackHugeIdleEnabled.get(idx, pdp.huge_idle);
